#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c5560ec9-32d6-514d-92fa-fbd77500d943" :question/math-academy-id "e-4431" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/bc/bcebda30c5b3a878271e607bc77f04612470812126f5916469147d884516dc20.png) find ![](images/e0/e0f9783da49e275b641d727e32506542449fe6be9db636fd1b6cc6007704b500.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given two ![](images/c1/c17e75a26e1dbfa0e89535380e33c9a232a5db7bb2841dec7a03b0fbe861b8c4.png) matrices ![](images/0d/0dffbd47bb64c877dbb545c2c5024e2e50ce9ec516b7a4509d53f77916e8c791.png) and ![](images/c4/c40e3405d51242d87b13698d1e715c1b391185890487e3d2a386cb09d3b952e9.png) we have\n\n![](images/aa/aae29aab049034fe50debd00d825e5e37b19de8f1a803dfb098727bbf8afe0e9.png)\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n![](images/bf/bf7493e53cfed1ef296dcc0f7020d7ceec014af63a0cbee3f8cac5e1a11afd0d.png)\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the ![](images/c2/c23fa2f99aef303059a834682e511943ffbe022294cf2d9ac8e72e707316a607.png)st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n![](images/b9/b968eb1cf6b8a72d8f4b46202ea1a6ab201145ae2531bfe2b5a2049aafae9b28.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "17487f8f-4974-5aaf-8729-6b1336ca5eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4432" :question/problem "Find $|A^{-1}|,$ if  ![](images/8a/8a5169c6a418a8ddaa9b3478e535039b253724ae622692ea728b2a94dc112e4a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that \n![](images/96/96eacd1217f27ffb734d64453ac3fca3cac6124a781dcd677c898ec4a4317427.png)\nfind \n![](images/64/64009b40661083c1e152b6277f329c6e22a0588fc0b3fd3d08db5e51ace0b27d.png)" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "702b6328-c32f-5527-b2ca-d3baaadb31ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263970} {:answer/id #uuid "7d75795f-2f71-5a1a-b8cc-d1f72992101b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-214" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264081} {:answer/id #uuid "9318164d-2060-5f48-886c-878e646e7c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264260} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0a2ee8-8586-50ff-ab69-638797faa787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264468} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a8cc04-1d7d-5562-8be8-65ee50e1bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9318164d-2060-5f48-886c-878e646e7c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264260} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d64a4d-a6ea-5c2c-a215-d5f4325e92b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a11bbff-ca26-5c49-a8ab-b5946f4034f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130493" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 7 \\\\ 4 & 8\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 7 \\\\ 4 & 8\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(40-28)\\cdot (6-24) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14df6deb-d3ee-5e1a-ad59-ff7cc32a5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263194} {:answer/id #uuid "5cad807e-0569-5ff3-9e40-730b8b912410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263801} {:answer/id #uuid "79d24e48-d809-52e3-ad2f-fd1e75e9882a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264056} {:answer/id #uuid "aac71a88-0dd8-5c6f-afdc-8df09b432ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264462} {:answer/id #uuid "ed152b32-61af-5f9e-b9dd-61f70984cef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14df6deb-d3ee-5e1a-ad59-ff7cc32a5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263194} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f7c60c4-fc52-503f-8ef4-67b53122d11d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42eb872e-48fc-5f57-9397-6ecc0f021c29" :question/math-academy-id "q-130529" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(-12+2)+(9-5)+(-6+20) \\\\  & =20+4+14 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "331f3993-b5ac-54a2-ac82-06dd0321b151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263442} {:answer/id #uuid "3603ab83-8aae-5dd5-89fa-afe1ef9f62d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263477} {:answer/id #uuid "57d3925a-c1e7-542d-8f33-1d3909394cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263757} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ead8f3-49e7-5a32-8b04-bc4291058f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264675} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e9d9c7-1fa1-50bc-b6dc-efdafdea5ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3603ab83-8aae-5dd5-89fa-afe1ef9f62d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263477} :answer-field/id #uuid "6691a242-c5c1-5177-b03a-f829842b5ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74270614-19f5-5cbe-a862-b299585b014f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130777" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a & b+2\\end{aligned}|=8$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a & b+2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{8}+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8+(-6+6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b298ccb-6d42-51e0-bec7-bdcce97b859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} {:answer/id #uuid "104af8d6-6df5-59fb-8200-c18490b4fd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327079} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8e5b8a-f047-5394-af88-102417a6f0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327338} {:answer/id #uuid "3fde6c7e-e80c-5ec0-964a-81bf5acc2144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327492} {:answer/id #uuid "c150ce04-23ed-5152-a4c5-802328f796f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b298ccb-6d42-51e0-bec7-bdcce97b859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} :answer-field/id #uuid "7775f4a0-2beb-5ee4-8f08-e48b58e45535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e713fddd-f608-51f9-ac5a-f16a9c918c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-42584" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(12-12)+(-15+24)+2(10-16) \\\\  & =0+9-12 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884c1925-0ccc-54e5-8235-15f4852ab5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} {:answer/id #uuid "974ae98b-f65f-5d56-abb2-235fcf5d1709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118810} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f4f7fc-49f1-51bb-a52f-e78439376e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118811} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c1329a-446a-56c8-94dc-f3d8fb69e271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118812} {:answer/id #uuid "665f03b3-8fe1-5194-816a-e67e96a71c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118813}] :answer-field/id #uuid "56464aea-61e2-5f04-a701-b6c9323c4b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118808}] :question/id #uuid "daa9792e-2290-5f44-bdfd-5cde9ef8123b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42996" :question/problem "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, find $det(A)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e097d8-e542-5acf-a330-fe6eeb6f27a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326952} {:answer/id #uuid "138116bf-b723-50e7-9f4c-0360ed73a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-434" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} {:answer/id #uuid "a0898f35-ae35-5a2c-85c3-2c1973fb0a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "870" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328324} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d333b3-316e-527a-9482-d8e7753af3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328390} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b3cbac-6be7-58e9-bfd2-408356f2bda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "138116bf-b723-50e7-9f4c-0360ed73a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-434" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327101} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a480665-7d5c-59ec-8577-d65fbb82b058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6ac6b8ae-6cc0-5802-bd63-e9b3982c4a3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43097" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107+0 & 215+0 & 99+1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =107\\cdot 8-215\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-434\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} {:answer/id #uuid "555a8298-192e-50be-8d44-14723e16f11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352323} {:answer/id #uuid "58619be6-c805-5344-9021-173a95976dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352344} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9db913-3c22-5c37-be9e-ca617e5d0dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353213} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c2b5b7-b8a6-51db-98b0-9f4ba4bb301f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd53aab-78d5-531e-a248-9e6ce0d0938b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31688145-09ed-5774-a522-d76e627c7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-88296" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
